  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: More possible showers today along and west of the Blue Ridge\nwill give way to dry weather to start the work week, with heat \nbuilding again towards the middle of this week.\n\nA backdoor front has well passed the region today, and is\ncurrently draped across the Carolinas. This has led to northeast\nflow across much of the region that looks to continue through\nMonday. Lingering moisture across the region coupled by mid\nlevel shortwave disturbances today will bring shower and\nthunderstorm chances to areas primarily along and west of the\nBlue Ridge today. The best chances look to remain along the Blue\nRidge where easterly/northeasterly flow will lead to upslope\nconditions across these areas. Overall, CAM guidance shows this\ncorridor of scattered showers developing through the late\nmorning and early afternoon hours today. In general, there will\nnot be enough instability to develop any strong deep convection\ntoday as SBCAPE values look to predominantly remain below 1000\nJ/Kg. Some gusty winds can't be ruled out with some of these\nshowers; however, as DCAPE values look to remain in the 800-1000\nJ/Kg range. \n\nBy Monday, surface high pressure and subsequently drier air\nlooks to settle further into the Mid-Atlantic states and the\nSoutheast, which will effectively cut off any precipitation\nchances through the early portion of this week. Temperatures\nalso look to moderate to near normal to slightly above normal\nvalues through Tuesday as an upper level trough keeps the upper\nlevel ridge over the central Conus to our west through this \nperiod. By Wednesday; however, the ridge looks to slide back \neast, and temperatures look to increase to above normal values\nonce again. Fortunately, current ensemble support keeps headline\nheat out of the area this week. \n\nAnother cold front looks to approach the region by the\nThursday/Friday timeframe; however, timing of this front is\nstill uncertain. After today's rainfall, this will be the next\nbest chance for rain across the region. Temperatures look to\ncool off once again to near to just above normal for this time\nof the year by next weekend.",
